{#
/**
 * @file
 * Vertical question tabs with one answer column per run.
 *
 * One tab per question down the left, that question's answers on the right. Used
 * by both AI Tester views: the comparison passes two sides per panel, the
 * single-run detail view passes one, and the panel grid takes its column count
 * from however many it gets. The tabs are hand-rolled rather than core's
 * #type => vertical_tabs, which is built for form 'details' groups and cannot
 * produce the tablist/tabpanel pairing this needs.
 *
 * Two accessibility decisions are load-bearing and must survive edits here:
 *
 * - No panel carries 'hidden' in the rendered markup. js/question-tabs.js adds it
 *   on attach, so with JavaScript unavailable the view degrades to a readable
 *   stacked list of every question instead of a blank right-hand column.
 * - Each tab's status is a visible text badge, not colour alone: the table this
 *   replaces had a Status column, and losing it would leave "differs" carried
 *   only by a chip colour.
 *
 * Available variables:
 * - tabs: List of tabs, each with:
 *   - id: The tab's own DOM id, referenced by its panel's aria-labelledby.
 *   - panel_id: The id of the panel this tab controls.
 *   - number: The question's position, already translated ("Question 3").
 *   - preview: A truncated copy of the question text.
 *   - status: The pair status machine name, used as a badge modifier class.
 *   - status_label: The human-readable status.
 * - panels: List of panels, each with:
 *   - id: The panel's DOM id, matching its tab's aria-controls.
 *   - tab_id: The controlling tab's id.
 *   - heading: The panel's headline, already composed as "Question N of M:
 *     <full question text>". The position is in the heading because the tab rail
 *     scrolls, so it is the only thing that says how far through a long question
 *     set the open panel is.
 *   - sides: List of per-run columns, each with content and an optional heading
 *     and meta. The comparison sets both, because a reader has to tell its two
 *     columns apart; the single-run view leaves them empty, since its page
 *     header already names the run. Empty rather than blank is deliberate — a
 *     heading element with no text in it is an accessibility defect.
 * - tablist_label: The accessible name for the tab list.
 * - empty: Message shown when there are no results to show.
 */
#}
{% if tabs is empty %}
  <p class="ys-qtabs__empty">{{ empty }}</p>
{% else %}
  <div class="ys-qtabs" data-ys-qtabs>
    <div class="ys-qtabs__list" role="tablist" aria-orientation="vertical" aria-label="{{ tablist_label }}">
      {% for tab in tabs %}
        <button type="button"
                class="ys-qtabs__tab"
                role="tab"
                id="{{ tab.id }}"
                aria-controls="{{ tab.panel_id }}"
                aria-selected="{{ loop.first ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
                tabindex="{{ loop.first ? '0' : '-1' }}">
          <span class="ys-qtabs__tab-number">{{ tab.number }}</span>
          <span class="ys-qtabs__tab-preview">{{ tab.preview }}</span>
          <span class="ys-compare-badge ys-compare-badge--{{ tab.status }}">{{ tab.status_label }}</span>
        </button>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <div class="ys-qtabs__panels">
      {% for panel in panels %}
        <div class="ys-qtabs__panel"
             role="tabpanel"
             id="{{ panel.id }}"
             aria-labelledby="{{ panel.tab_id }}"
             tabindex="0">
          {# h2/h3, not h3/h4: the route title is the page's only h1 and neither
             view emits an h2 of its own, so starting at h3 would skip a level
             for anyone navigating by heading. #}
          <h2 class="ys-qtabs__question">{{ panel.heading }}</h2>
          <div class="ys-qtabs__sides">
            {% for side in panel.sides %}
              <div class="ys-qtabs__side">
                {% if side.heading %}
                  <h3 class="ys-qtabs__side-heading">{{ side.heading }}</h3>
                {% endif %}
                {% if side.meta %}
                  <p class="ys-qtabs__side-meta">{{ side.meta }}</p>
                {% endif %}
                {{ side.content }}
              </div>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
{% endif %}
